From: RDK Management
Subject: Do not request FLAT_BINDER_FLAG_TXN_SECURITY_CTX on AppArmor devices

This device's kernel enforces AppArmor (CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR), not
SELinux. AppArmor's apparmor_secid_to_secctx() returns -EINVAL for any
secid that has no registered label (e.g. unconfined processes), and
binder_transaction() hard-fails every transaction to a node with
txn_security_ctx set if that lookup fails (drivers/android/binder.c,
"transaction failed .../-22 ... line 2861").

Registering the context manager without the flag avoids requiring a
security context this kernel/LSM combination can never provide, so
every client's addService()/checkService() call to servicemanager was
failing with EX_TRANSACTION_FAILED (-129).

diff --git a/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp b/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
+++ b/libs/binder/ProcessState.cpp
@@ -192,7 +192,15 @@ bool ProcessState::becomeContextManager()
     AutoMutex _l(mLock);

     flat_binder_object obj {
-        .flags = FLAT_BINDER_FLAG_TXN_SECURITY_CTX,
+        // Do not request FLAT_BINDER_FLAG_TXN_SECURITY_CTX: this device's
+        // kernel enforces AppArmor (CONFIG_SECURITY_APPARMOR), not SELinux.
+        // AppArmor's apparmor_secid_to_secctx() returns -EINVAL for any
+        // secid that has no registered label (e.g. unconfined processes),
+        // and binder_transaction() hard-fails every transaction to a node
+        // with txn_security_ctx set if that lookup fails. Registering
+        // without the flag avoids requiring a security context this
+        // kernel/LSM combination can't provide.
+        .flags = 0,
     };

     int result = ioctl(mDriverFD, BINDER_SET_CONTEXT_MGR_EXT, &obj);
